{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","statement":"Tutorial 5 begins where Tutorial 4 left off, and introduces creating automated tests for the web-poll application.","source_span":"This tutorial begins where :doc:`Tutorial 4 </intro/tutorial04>` left off. We've built a web-poll application, and we'll now create some automated tests for it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","statement":"Jacob Kaplan-Moss, one of Django's original developers, said \"Code without tests is broken by design.\"","source_span":"Jacob Kaplan-Moss, one of Django's original developers, says \"Code without tests is broken by design.\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","statement":"The Question.was_published_recently() method incorrectly returns True if the Question's pub_date is in the future, not just when published within the last day.","source_span":"the ``Question.was_published_recently()`` method returns ``True`` if the ``Question`` was published within the last day (which is correct) but also if the ``Question``’s ``pub_date`` field is in the future (which certainly isn't)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","statement":"In the shell demonstration, a Question object with pub_date set 30 days in the future returns True from was_published_recently(), confirming the bug.","source_span":">>> future_question = Question(pub_date=timezone.now() + datetime.timedelta(days=30))\n    >>> # was it published recently?\n    >>> future_question.was_published_recently()\n    True"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What bug exists in the Question.was_published_recently() method described in part 5 of the Django tutorial?","answer":"It returns True even when the Question's pub_date is in the future, when it should only return True for Questions published within the last day."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What quote about testing is attributed to Jacob Kaplan-Moss in the Django tutorial?","answer":"\"Code without tests is broken by design.\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[3],"question":"In the Django shell example used to confirm the bug, how many days in the future is the future_question's pub_date set?","answer":"30 days in the future."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Which prior tutorial part does Django tutorial part 5 continue from?","answer":"It continues from Tutorial 4."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What value does was_published_recently() return for a Question with a future pub_date in the tutorial's shell test?","answer":"It returns True, which is incorrect since a future date is not \"recent.\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial05","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the main topic introduced in part 5 of the Django tutorial?","answer":"Automated testing for the web-poll application."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"Tutorial 6 begins where Tutorial 5 left off and adds a stylesheet and an image to the polls app.","source_span":"This tutorial begins where :doc:`Tutorial 5 </intro/tutorial05>` left off. We've built a tested web-poll application, and we'll now add a stylesheet and an image."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"django.contrib.staticfiles collects static files from each application into a single location that can easily be served in production.","source_span":"That's what ``django.contrib.staticfiles`` is for: it collects static files from each of your applications (and any other places you specify) into a single location that can easily be served in production."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"AppDirectoriesFinder, one of the default STATICFILES_FINDERS, looks for a \"static\" subdirectory in each of the INSTALLED_APPS.","source_span":"One of the defaults is ``AppDirectoriesFinder`` which looks for a \"static\" subdirectory in each of the :setting:`INSTALLED_APPS`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The polls stylesheet should be created at the path polls/static/polls/style.css.","source_span":"your stylesheet should be at ``polls/static/polls/style.css``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The stylesheet code sets the link color of list-item links to green: \"li a { color: green; }\".","source_span":"li a {\n        color: green;\n    }"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The {% load static %} tag and {% static 'polls/style.css' %} are added to polls/templates/polls/index.html to load the stylesheet.","source_span":"{% load static %}\n\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"{% static 'polls/style.css' %}\">"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The background image file used in the tutorial is named background.png, with full path polls/static/polls/images/background.png.","source_span":"we're using a file named ``background.png``, which will have the full path ``polls/static/polls/images/background.png``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The CSS added for the background image is: body { background: white url(\"images/background.png\") no-repeat; }.","source_span":"body {\n        background: white url(\"images/background.png\") no-repeat;\n    }"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"The {% static %} template tag is not available in static files not generated by Django, so relative paths should always be used to link static files to each other.","source_span":"The ``{% static %}`` template tag is not available for use in static files which aren't generated by Django, like your stylesheet. You should always use **relative paths** to link your static files between each other"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","statement":"Part 7 of the tutorial teaches how to customize Django's automatically-generated admin site.","source_span":"read :doc:`part 7 of this tutorial </intro/tutorial07>` to learn how to customize Django's automatically-generated admin site."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the full file path for the polls stylesheet created in part 6 of the Django tutorial?","answer":"polls/static/polls/style.css."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What CSS rule is added to make the polls question links green in the Django tutorial?","answer":"\"li a { color: green; }\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the purpose of django.contrib.staticfiles according to the Django tutorial?","answer":"It collects static files from each of an app's applications into a single location that can easily be served in production."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the full path of the background image file used in part 6 of the Django tutorial?","answer":"polls/static/polls/images/background.png."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[8],"question":"Why does the tutorial recommend using relative paths to link static files to each other?","answer":"Because the {% static %} template tag isn't available in static files not generated by Django, and relative paths let you change STATIC_URL without modifying many file paths."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial06","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What topic does part 7 of the Django tutorial cover, according to part 6?","answer":"How to customize Django's automatically-generated admin site."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"Tutorial 7 begins where Tutorial 6 left off and focuses on customizing Django's automatically-generated admin site.","source_span":"This tutorial begins where :doc:`Tutorial 6 </intro/tutorial06>` left off. We're continuing the web-poll application and will focus on customizing Django's automatically-generated admin site"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"Setting fields = [\"pub_date\", \"question_text\"] on the QuestionAdmin class reorders the edit form so Publication date comes before Question text.","source_span":"class QuestionAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n        fields = [\"pub_date\", \"question_text\"]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"The fieldsets example splits the Question admin form into a None-titled fieldset containing question_text and a \"Date information\" fieldset containing pub_date.","source_span":"fieldsets = [\n            (None, {\"fields\": [\"question_text\"]}),\n            (\"Date information\", {\"fields\": [\"pub_date\"]}),\n        ]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"A ChoiceInline defined as admin.StackedInline with extra = 3 provides three extra slots for related Choice objects on the Question admin page by default.","source_span":"class ChoiceInline(admin.StackedInline):\n        model = Choice\n        extra = 3"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"Changing ChoiceInline from admin.StackedInline to admin.TabularInline displays the related Choice objects in a more compact, table-based format.","source_span":"With that ``TabularInline`` (instead of ``StackedInline``), the related objects are displayed in a more compact, table-based format"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"The list_display admin option is a list of field names to display as columns on the change list page for an object.","source_span":"the :attr:`~django.contrib.admin.ModelAdmin.list_display` admin option, which is a list of field names to display, as columns, on the change list page for the object"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","statement":"Every ForeignKey relationship in the Django admin gets an \"Add another question\" button, shown as a plus sign, for free.","source_span":"Also note the \"Add another question\" button (displayed as a plus sign) to the right of the \"Question\" field. Every ``ForeignKey`` relationship gets this button for free."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How do you reorder the Question admin edit form so Publication date appears before Question text, per part 7 of the Django tutorial?","answer":"By setting fields = [\"pub_date\", \"question_text\"] on the QuestionAdmin class."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How many extra Choice slots does the ChoiceInline with extra = 3 provide on the Question admin page?","answer":"Three extra slots for related Choices."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What change makes the Choice inline display switch from a stacked layout to a more compact, table-based format?","answer":"Changing ChoiceInline from admin.StackedInline to admin.TabularInline."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What does the list_display admin option control in the Django admin change list page?","answer":"It's a list of field names shown as columns on the change list page for an object."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What button does every ForeignKey relationship get for free in the Django admin, according to tutorial part 7?","answer":"An \"Add another question\" button, displayed as a plus sign, next to the related field."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial07","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the two fieldsets created when splitting the Question admin form, and which fields does each contain?","answer":"A fieldset with no title containing question_text, and a \"Date information\" fieldset containing pub_date."}
